A||||17||Eric||Sore Butt
Z||000000||Eric||06-12-99||07:18 PM||||Does anybody have seats from a different car in their Fiero? I have been considering recovering the seats in leather when I get the time and money, but lately I have been wondering if I could save some trouble and just replace the seats with a pair from a junked Camaro or Firebird.  I want to put a set of racing harnesses in the car and realized I will have to modify the original seats in order to get the proper spacing for the belts around, or in this case through, the headrest.  I don't think Mr. Mike makes a kit for that application.  If the seats are narrow enough, I don't think it would be a problem to adapt them to fit on the slider.  After all, it's not the seats that hold you when you get in a crash, it's the belts.  Any ideas?  If you managed to get the racing harness to work with your seats, please tell me how.  
Z||000001||Eric||06-12-99||07:21 PM||||I don't really have a problem with a sore ass.  Hope no one else does either.  I just thought that would get some attention.
Z||000002||FireWalker||06-13-99||11:38 AM||||On my way ( in a week or two) Home to North Carolina with my newly aquired Fiero, from Cleveland, Ohio.  Was hoping your post would have the # of cushions needed to make it a comfortible ride..that's 12 hours of bumps! =o)<br>
Z||000003||FastTrack||06-23-99||04:58 AM||||A friend of mine is lending me the passenger seat out of his z24 to try a swap.  I'm assuming I will have to use my Fiero's seat rails (I don't think there are too many seats made with rails that low to the floor).  I should be trying the swap on Thursday.  I'll let you know what happens.
Z||000004||Bozone||06-23-99||11:32 AM||||I always thought my Fiero was comfortable to drive. I took it over 3,000 miles in a weeks time and my ass never did get sore - not like when I make a long run in my Toyota truck, which gets to my lower back. You guys are spoilt!! :-)
Z||000005||Carrolles||07-01-99||11:46 AM||||My wife and I drove about 1300 miles round trip to attend the Daytona Fiero Festival.  We both found the Fiero seats very comfortable for the whole trip.<br>
Z||000006||Eric||07-01-99||12:33 PM||||I wasn't saying the stock seats are bad, I just would like to install some racing harnesses and the headrest on the seat won't allow proper spacing of the shoulder belts.  I would  get seats from a different car and sell my old seats, which are in good condition, to someone who needs them.  That way everybody is happy.
Z||000007||ChadMan||07-02-99||07:01 AM||||What happened with the Z24 seats, FastTrack?  I ask because the padding in the driver's seat of my 86 SE is shot and I was considering swapping in some Sunbird or Sunfire seats and wondered if I can just bolt 'em in, or will I need to use the Fiero seat rails.
Z||000008||FastTrack||07-16-99||05:23 PM||||Sorry about the wait...  My friend with the Z24 ended up having to work and we weren't able to try the swap that day, and my car went in to get painted the day after.  I finally did get to try the seats and it worked fine.  To my surprise when I put the two seats next to each other, they are almost identical.  I would imagine that alot of other GM seats would probably fit (using the fiero's original rails of course).
Z||000009||Eric||07-16-99||06:34 PM||||That's good news.  When I get some cash, I'll try a seat swap with some newer seats.
Z||000010||ChadMan||07-19-99||07:13 AM||||That is good news.  I just ordered a driver's seat for my 88 coupe from Kick Hill Farm.  The 88 is in excellent condition except for a torn driver's seat, so I wanted to keep it all original.<p>The 86 SE is another story - it looks nice, but is showing its age, so I'll take the cheapest seat that looks nice and is comfortable.  Junkyard, here I come!
Z||000011||DJRice||07-19-99||02:58 PM||||I have a pair of seats from a 97 camaro in my 84.  I had to take the rails off of the old seats and put them on the new seats, but they  fit.  They sit a bit higher then the old seats because they are 6 way adjustable, but they are nice.  Everything bolts right up.
Z||000012||JetroGT||07-19-99||11:40 PM||||If the seats from the Camaro and some other GM made cars are almost identical to the Fiero seats, why not just use the covers insted of unbolting the hardware?
Z||000013||lowCG||07-20-99||12:13 AM||||Hmmm,some of those 80's Vette seats weren't too bad either,some were leather also.
Z||000014||ChadMan||07-20-99||07:34 AM||||I just got my 88 driver's seat from Kick Hill Farm, and man, it's nice.  The foam padding is in great shape, too. It looks like it's only been sat in for about 25K miles by a 95-lb woman.  It wasn't cheap ($120 incl. shipping) but it was a lot cheaper than having my existing seat recovered here in town.  No more cheesy fake-fur Auto Zone seat cover!
Z||000015||batboy||07-20-99||09:47 AM||||ChadMan, what is Kick Hill Farm? Do they specialize in Fiero parts? If so, do they have a web site or 800 number?
Z||000016||DJRice||07-20-99||12:45 PM||||The main reason I didnt just take the covers off is because changing the rails involved 4 bolts to get the seat out of the car, and 4 more bolts to replace the rails, It just seemed a whole lot easier.
Z||000017||ChadMan||07-21-99||08:38 AM||||Kick Hill Farm is a salvage yard that specializes in Fieros.  It's run by a guy named Bob Parker.  Here's their web site:<p>www.kickhill.com/fiero.html
